

**21. Spheroidal Wave Functions**

**Mathematical Properties**

**21.1. Definition of Elliptical Coordinates**

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.1.1}\qquad\underline{\xi}=\frac{r_{1}+r_{2}}{2f},\;\;\eta=\frac{r_{1}-r_{2}}{2f}\end{array}\]

\(r_{1}\) and \(r_{2}\) are the distances to the foci of a family of confocal ellipses and hyperbolas; \(2f\) is the distance between foci.

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.1.2}\qquad\underline{a}=\underline{fk},\;b=\underline{f}/\sqrt{\xi^{2}-1},\qquad\underline{e}=\frac{f}{\alpha}\end{array}\]

\(a=\)semi-major axis; \(\underline{b}=\)semi-minor axis; \(\epsilon=\)eccentricity.

Equation of Family of Confocal Ellipses**

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.1.3}\qquad\underline{\frac{x^{2}}{\xi^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{\xi^{2}-1}}=f^{2}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{(1<\xi<\infty)}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{\text{Equation of Family of Confocal Hyperbolas}}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.1.4}\qquad\underline{\frac{x^{2}}{\eta^{2}}\frac{y^{2}}{1-\eta^{2}}}=f^{2}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} \left(-1<\eta<1\right)\end{array}\]

Relations Between Cartesian and Elliptical Coordinates**

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.1.5}\qquad x=\underline{f\xi\eta};\;y=\underline{f}\sqrt{(\xi^{2}-1)(1-\eta^{2})}\end{array}\]

### 21.2. Definition of Prolate Spheroidal Coordinates

If the system of confocal ellipses and hyberbolas referred to in 21.1.3 and 21.1.4 revolves around the major axis, then

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.2.1}\qquad\underline{\frac{x^{2}}{\xi^{2}}+\frac{r^{2}}{\xi^{2}-1}}=f^{2};\;\;\;\frac{x^{2}}{\eta^{2}}\frac{r^{2}}{1-\eta^{2}}=f^{2}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} y=r\cos\phi;\;z=r\sin\phi;\;0\leq\phi\leq 2\pi\end{array}\]

where \(\xi,\eta\)and \(\phi\) are prolate spheroidal coordinates.

**Relations Between Cartesian and Prolate Spheroidal Coordinates**

\[\begin{array}{l} \underline{21.3.1}\qquad\underline{\frac{r^{2}}{\xi^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{\xi^{2}-1}}=f^{2};\;\;\;\;\frac{r^{2}}{\eta^{2}}\frac{y^{2}}{1-\eta^{2}}=f^{2}\end{array}\]

\[\begin{array}{l} z=\underline{r\cos\phi};\;x=r\sin\phi;\;0\leq\phi\leq 2\pi\end{array}\]

where \(\xi,\eta\)and \(\phi\) are oblate spheroidal coordinates.

